# TEMPLATE. Not applied by any kustomization, on purpose.
#
# This is the one secret in this layer, and it is created once by hand because
# it must not be guessable and must not be in the repository in plaintext.
# Copy this file to grafana-admin.sops.yaml, put a real password in it, encrypt
# it, and add it to ../controllers/kustomization.yaml.
#
#   1. Generate a password you will not remember and do not need to:
#
#        head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64
#
#   2. Copy this file and replace the placeholder:
#
#        cp grafana-admin.sops.yaml.example grafana-admin.sops.yaml
#
#   3. Encrypt it in place, to the cluster's age recipient:
#
#        sops --encrypt --in-place \
#             --age <the age public key this cluster decrypts with> \
#             --encrypted-regex '^(data|stringData)$' \
#             grafana-admin.sops.yaml
#
#   4. Uncomment the entry in kustomization.yaml IN THIS DIRECTORY, and commit
#      BOTH changes together.
#
#      Not ../controllers/kustomization.yaml -- kustomize refuses to load a
#      file from outside its own directory, so ../controllers references this
#      DIRECTORY instead. An earlier version of this instruction named the file
#      and could not be followed at all.
#
# WHAT HAPPENS IF YOU SKIP THIS: Grafana does not start. Its deployment
# references this secret by name, so the pod stays pending on a missing
# reference until you create it.
#
# That is the intended behaviour, not an oversight. The alternative — a chart
# default of admin/admin, or a generated password stored in the release — is an
# observability dashboard that comes up with a credential somebody else also
# knows, in front of every log line and every trace on the cluster. A component
# that will not start is a problem you fix this afternoon. A component that
# started with a known password is a problem you find out about later.
#
# ROTATION: replace the value, re-encrypt, and let the reconcile apply it.
# Grafana reads the admin credential at start-up, so restart the pod
# afterwards; changing the secret alone does not change the running password.
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: grafana-admin
  namespace: observability
type: Opaque
stringData:
  admin-user: admin
  # REPLACE THIS. If you can read this line in a repository, it is not a
  # password — and `sops` on an unmodified file will happily encrypt the
  # placeholder, producing something that looks encrypted and is not secret.
  admin-password: replace-me-before-encrypting
